1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,230
AI agents need to pay for data and tools.

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:06,070
But with a separate wallet for every agent,

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:08,110
who keeps the whole team on budget?

4
00:00:08,550 --> 00:00:11,930
I built Orbit to give them one shared treasury on NEAR,

5
00:00:12,210 --> 00:00:14,390
with spending limits enforced by the contract.

6
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:16,100
Let me show you.

7
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:19,700
Here are our available funds and today’s allowance.

8
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:22,800
I’ll scroll down to the agents sharing that budget.

9
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:26,380
I’ll open research-scout.

10
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,780
Its key can only request payments,

11
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:31,295
with limits for each payment, the agent,

12
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:32,740
and the shared workspace.

13
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,840
Follow its activity, choose Paid, and open the account check.

14
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:48,640
Here’s what the payment bought: an account access report.

15
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,835
It’s reconstructed at the saved delivery block; the original response

16
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:53,840
wasn’t preserved.

17
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,360
Now let’s look at a separate recorded request.

18
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:08,120
It hit the operations workspace cap.

19
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:11,360
Open it, and we can see why it was rejected.

20
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:13,040
No tokens went out.

21
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:20,500
That’s Orbit: useful work and a shared spending boundary,

22
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:21,620
in one place.

23
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:25,600
This walkthrough uses no-value test tokens on NEAR testnet.
